00:02:45.910 --> 00:02:50.100
not devoting it to other things that are
important to you,

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:51.640
then obviously things are out of whack.

00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:54.640
I, a couple of things I backed into.

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:55.950
You're in the joint program.

00:02:55.950 --> 00:02:57.143
Business law program.

00:02:57.143 --> 00:02:59.555
I, I came from Brigham Young University to
Harvard.

00:02:59.555 --> 00:03:02.350
And was convinced I would flunk out.

00:03:02.350 --> 00:03:03.850
And cuz I looked around and

00:03:03.850 --> 00:03:08.430
I saw all these people who were obviously
smarter than me and and how was I possibly

00:03:08.430 --> 00:03:11.310
gonna make it in this environment except
by just studying like crazy.

00:03:11.310 --> 00:03:13.420
And so, I studied all the time.

00:03:13.420 --> 00:03:18.330
And if I was not studying, I felt like
there's this black cloud hanging over me.

00:03:18.330 --> 00:03:19.800
I should be studying.

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:22.560
I've gotta be working because I'm gonna,
I'm gonna flunk out.

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:26.760
And, and it was, it was omnipresent and at
some point I finally said you know what,

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:30.780
I'm, I'm gonna do something which, which
goes back to biblical times,

00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:32.220
I'm gonna take Sunday off.

00:03:32.220 --> 00:03:36.575
I'm gonna decide I'm not gonna study at
all on Sunday, and

00:03:36.575 --> 00:03:42.512
I'm gonna devote that day to my family, to
worship and just personal time.

00:03:42.512 --> 00:03:46.721
And it was amazing what happened when I
made that decision because then,

00:03:46.721 --> 00:03:50.179
on that Sunday, I didn't feel the black
cloud there anymore.

00:03:50.179 --> 00:03:54.890
It's like, okay I, I, I can't study today,
I don't have to worry about it.

00:03:54.890 --> 00:03:57.980
And, and the same thing happened as, as I
went into my career,

00:03:57.980 --> 00:04:00.060
in the consulting industry.

00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:00.840
I said, you know what?

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:04.540
I, I'm not just gonna work when I come at
the end of the day.

00:04:04.540 --> 00:04:05.530
It may be a late night.

00:04:05.530 --> 00:04:06.860
It may be I get home at 6:00 or 7:00
instead.

00:04:06.860 --> 00:04:11.940
But when I come home I'm gonna close my
brief, briefcase and not work.

00:04:11.940 --> 00:04:15.130
And I'm gonna devote the time I have at
home to my family.

00:04:15.130 --> 00:04:16.650
And it was wonderful.

00:04:16.650 --> 00:04:21.350
It was just, it was, it was freeing
because I could really focus on

00:04:21.350 --> 00:04:25.650
the things that I cared most about in life
which, which were my wife and my kids.

00:04:25.650 --> 00:04:29.520
And now of course if there was a big
presentation coming up why I'd, you know,

00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:30.300
I'd break that rule.

00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:36.490
But, in terms of a, a regular pattern of
life those were a couple of things I did.

00:04:36.490 --> 00:04:39.440
Sundays stayed, for me, a day of family.

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:41.930
Coming home at the end of the day stayed a
family time.

00:04:41.930 --> 00:04:42.820
I traveled a good deal.

00:04:42.820 --> 00:04:47.050
Of course, if I was, was on the road I
worked like crazy late into the night.

00:04:47.050 --> 00:04:52.398
But a few of those decisions early on
shaped how I spent my time and probably

00:04:52.398 --> 00:04:57.679
helped me balance my life to, towards
those things that mattered most to me.

00:04:57.679 --> 00:04:59.429
&gt;&gt; So how did you get away with that?

00:04:59.429 --> 00:05:01.709
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I mean, there's a lot of people,

00:05:01.709 --> 00:05:06.097
I mean, all of us come from these, you
know, careers or are going into these

00:05:06.097 --> 00:05:10.512
careers where, if you say, I'm gonna go
home at 6 o'clock, I'm sorry.

00:05:10.512 --> 00:05:13.679
That, you know, that's not always met with
a lot of positive reception.

00:05:13.679 --> 00:05:16.399
&gt;&gt; Yeah, no.
I, I, and I may have misspoken there.

00:05:16.399 --> 00:05:19.262
Some nights, I might have been able to get
home at seven.

00:05:19.262 --> 00:05:19.887
&gt;&gt; Or take Sunday off.

00:05:19.887 --> 00:05:25.020
&gt;&gt; And, and, but, but I but I found if you
take a block of time off for

00:05:25.020 --> 00:05:29.470
yourself you may well be more productive
than if you don't.

00:05:29.470 --> 00:05:32.887
And and that may not be true depending on
the organization you go to.

00:05:32.887 --> 00:05:36.716
But I remember when I was talking to Bill
Bain about joining Bain and Company and

00:05:36.716 --> 00:05:39.760
I said look I, I have to take all day
Sunday off.

00:05:39.760 --> 00:05:42.370
So if there's like a company meeting, or
if you want to come in for

00:05:42.370 --> 00:05:45.330
a case team meetings on Sunday, I just
won't be there.

00:05:45.330 --> 00:05:46.450
And if that's something that,

00:05:46.450 --> 00:05:50.910
that the firm can't accept, then I'm
probably not the right guy for the firm.

00:05:50.910 --> 00:05:52.640
And, and I live by that.

00:05:52.640 --> 00:05:55.920
A, again, unless there was some kind of a,
an unusual experience, some,

00:05:55.920 --> 00:05:57.360
you know, terrible crisis happened.

00:05:57.360 --> 00:05:59.810
I was going to jump in with both feet like
everybody else.

00:05:59.810 --> 00:06:03.330
But that was the every day occurrence, and
I think it may be more effective and

00:06:03.330 --> 00:06:03.950
more productive.

00:06:03.950 --> 00:06:07.910
And I, I had good consulting assignments
and got promoted as time went on.

00:06:07.910 --> 00:06:11.390
So I don't think it hurts to have
something more

00:06:11.390 --> 00:06:13.019
in your life than just work.

00:06:14.050 --> 00:06:18.090
I think, I think having faith, or a
community that you care about,

00:06:18.090 --> 00:06:20.230
politics, and children.

00:06:20.230 --> 00:06:23.050
I think that makes you a more full human
being,

00:06:23.050 --> 00:06:26.900
more able to understand how the world
works, and how most people think, and

00:06:26.900 --> 00:06:28.250
may actually make you more effective.

00:06:28.250 --> 00:06:31.650
And by the way if it doesn't, and you
don't get promoted

00:06:31.650 --> 00:06:35.330
in the way you wanted to And you don't
make as much money as you wanted to.

00:06:35.330 --> 00:06:38.393
So what?
Life is not about getting promoted and

00:06:38.393 --> 00:06:39.036
money.

00:06:39.036 --> 00:06:41.780
If that's how you measure your life, I got
some bad news.

00:06:41.780 --> 00:06:43.730
There's serendipity in the world.

00:06:43.730 --> 00:06:45.540
Bad things happen in business and the
economy.

00:06:45.540 --> 00:06:51.100
You can't be guaranteed you're gonna get
promoted, and make a lot of money.

00:06:51.100 --> 00:06:55.010
But if you measure yourself by the things
that count most to you, your relationship

00:06:55.010 --> 00:06:59.400
with your spouse, your friendships, your
children, your family, those things

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:03.430
you can succeed at whether or not the
world goes to hell in a handbasket.

00:07:03.430 --> 00:07:08.044
So you know, I think you lay out how you
want to live your life and, and you do

00:07:08.044 --> 00:07:12.179
that you can have success regardless of
what happens in the world around you.

00:07:12.179 --> 00:07:16.091
&gt;&gt; You mentioned your time at Harvard and
how this kind of came to you then.

00:07:16.091 --> 00:07:19.320
This, this need to create some sectors in
your life.

00:07:19.320 --> 00:07:20.800
One of the other decisions you made at
Harvard was,

00:07:20.800 --> 00:07:26.190
you're graduating with a JDMBA, you
decided to go into management consulting.

00:07:26.190 --> 00:07:30.200
I'm curious for all of us making these
kind of choices today if you Have similar

00:07:30.200 --> 00:07:32.569
interests, and were here where we are
today,

00:07:32.569 --> 00:07:36.054
would you make the same decision to go
into management consulting?

00:07:36.054 --> 00:07:40.930
And I ask that as a JDMBA going into
management consulting,

00:07:40.930 --> 00:07:43.179
so I hope the answer is yes.

00:07:43.179 --> 00:07:43.762
&gt;&gt; My condolences.

00:07:43.762 --> 00:07:44.970
&gt;&gt; Yeah, no, thank you.

00:07:44.970 --> 00:07:52.654
&gt;&gt; No, I mean, my path, was very different
than the success books suggested.

00:07:52.654 --> 00:07:56.170
I mean there are books out there that said
that you know you

00:07:56.170 --> 00:07:59.904
ought to have a clear goal in mind and
think about that goal and

00:07:59.904 --> 00:08:03.072
and I grew up in Detroit My dad was a car
company CEO.

00:08:03.072 --> 00:08:07.330
And I fully anticipated to go work for a
co, for an automobile company.

00:08:07.330 --> 00:08:08.910
That's what I wanted to do.

00:08:08.910 --> 00:08:12.270
And so after my first year in the JDMP
program,

00:08:12.270 --> 00:08:14.510
I went to work at Chrysler Corporation.

00:08:14.510 --> 00:08:15.820
And thinking that's where I was gonna go.

00:08:15.820 --> 00:08:17.350
And I hated it.

00:08:17.350 --> 00:08:19.450
I was so deep in the organization.

00:08:19.450 --> 00:08:23.120
And of course the people, I mean, my boss'
boss' boss had never met the CEO and

00:08:23.120 --> 00:08:24.420
never would.

00:08:24.420 --> 00:08:27.680
And and decisions being made that would
affect the success of that company,

00:08:27.680 --> 00:08:32.320
I'd never have any impact on unless I was
there 50 or some odd years.

00:08:32.320 --> 00:08:36.480
And I thought boy, this is just not at all
like I imagined it.

00:08:36.480 --> 00:08:40.760
And, and so I came back in the second
year, and, got a job.

00:08:40.760 --> 00:08:43.730
I think it was my second year, in the
program.

00:08:43.730 --> 00:08:47.456
I got a job with the Boston Consulting
Group, for a summer job.

00:08:47.456 --> 00:08:51.050
And it was fascinating, and it was
exciting.

00:08:51.050 --> 00:08:52.360
And I loved it.

00:08:52.360 --> 00:08:55.860
And, and so it was not a great analysis I
did to say,

00:08:55.860 --> 00:08:57.740
this is the right next step for my career.

00:08:57.740 --> 00:08:58.570
I just enjoyed it.

00:08:58.570 --> 00:09:01.720
My path in life has primarily been focused
on doing things I thought were fun and

00:09:01.720 --> 00:09:04.590
enjoyable, and so that was fun.

00:09:04.590 --> 00:09:08.910
My undergraduate major was English.

00:09:10.380 --> 00:09:12.900
Why would you go into English?

00:09:12.900 --> 00:09:18.236
There's no future, right, as an English
major?

00:09:18.236 --> 00:09:20.094
What are you going to do, all right?

00:09:20.094 --> 00:09:22.020
But I liked reading and I liked writing.

00:09:22.020 --> 00:09:25.790
So I went, I took English as my major, and
then coming out of business school,

00:09:25.790 --> 00:09:29.630
law school I went into consulting because
I enjoyed it,

00:09:29.630 --> 00:09:31.550
not because that's where I thought I'd
spend my life.

00:09:31.550 --> 00:09:35.912
I expected I'd be there for two or three
years, like most people do, and

00:09:35.912 --> 00:09:39.271
then get a job in a line corporation of
some kind and, and

00:09:39.271 --> 00:09:43.876
perhaps move up, more aggressively by
having started in consulting.

00:09:43.876 --> 00:09:45.532
I love consulting.

00:09:45.532 --> 00:09:49.180
Because I am, I am oriented towards
solving problems.

00:09:49.180 --> 00:09:52.760
I like analysis and data and problem
solving and writing and

00:09:52.760 --> 00:09:54.050
writing presentations.

00:09:54.050 --> 00:09:54.900
That's what I like to do.

00:09:54.900 --> 00:09:56.100
That's what took me there.

00:09:56.100 --> 00:10:00.680
And so, I would, I would go to those, I've
followed

00:10:00.680 --> 00:10:05.190
the career path that you enjoy most as
opposed to trying to follow a career path

00:10:05.190 --> 00:10:10.310
that you think will lead to the highest
income or the quickest promotion.

00:10:10.310 --> 00:10:16.450
Do what you enjoy and then your life will
be enjoyable and fulfilling.

00:10:16.450 --> 00:10:19.460
&gt;&gt; So, one other thing you're known for

00:10:19.460 --> 00:10:22.610
is parachuting into very troubled
environments.

00:10:22.610 --> 00:10:27.120
You went from being captain back to being
consulting to turn that around.

00:10:27.120 --> 00:10:30.400
You went from Bain Cap to the Olympics,
turned that around, and

00:10:30.400 --> 00:10:34.120
then you parachuted into my home state of
Massachusetts and

00:10:34.120 --> 00:10:37.130
helped turn that around as well.

00:10:37.130 --> 00:10:39.088
What, what led you to make those
decisions?

00:10:39.088 --> 00:10:43.687
What were you looking at to make to make
those kind of like pretty risky bets.

00:10:43.687 --> 00:10:48.182
&gt;&gt; Yeah I, I don't know that I have jumped
into troubled situations

00:10:48.182 --> 00:10:50.896
because I enjoy troubled situations.

00:10:50.896 --> 00:10:56.490
[LAUGH] I, I, but but it is a sense of
obligation.

00:10:56.490 --> 00:11:00.300
And maybe it's my upbringing or my faith.

00:11:00.300 --> 00:11:02.720
Or I say upbringing in my parents.

00:11:02.720 --> 00:11:06.940
My dad had this sense of obligation to the
country.

00:11:06.940 --> 00:11:10.250
And my dad was born in Mexico, of American
parents living there.

00:11:11.320 --> 00:11:13.420
There was a revolution at the time.

00:11:13.420 --> 00:11:14.490
Came back to the United States.

00:11:14.490 --> 00:11:19.170
Lived in public housing, got public
assistance, and and grew up poor,

00:11:19.170 --> 00:11:20.540
very poor.

00:11:20.540 --> 00:11:23.450
And had a, a, perhaps as a result of that,

00:11:23.450 --> 00:11:27.720
of bringing in the opportunity in this
life, th, that developed over his life,

00:11:27.720 --> 00:11:30.936
had a great sense of obligation to America
and to the community.

00:11:30.936 --> 00:11:35.110
And so, whenever he felt that there was a
need that wasn't being met,

00:11:35.110 --> 00:11:36.670
he volunteered and jumped in.

00:11:36.670 --> 00:11:39.810
And, and somehow, I felt the same way.

00:11:39.810 --> 00:11:44.300
So, in, the first step, you mentioned
going from Bain Capital, which was highly

00:11:44.300 --> 00:11:49.380
successful and growing like crazy, and
Bain Consulting was in trouble and

00:11:49.380 --> 00:11:52.309
it was in trouble because of financial
steps that had been taken by the founders.

00:11:53.350 --> 00:11:56.560
And it looked like it might disappear all
together.

00:11:56.560 --> 00:11:59.770
And I was asked by the partners of the
consulting firm if I would leave

00:11:59.770 --> 00:12:05.140
Bain Capital for a couple years, and come
back, and run the consulting firm.

00:12:05.140 --> 00:12:07.284
And I felt like how, how can I say no?

00:12:07.284 --> 00:12:11.004
There were a thousand people who were
working at Bain Consulting at that point,

00:12:11.004 --> 00:12:14.160
and I figured that there was a very high
risk it wouldn't make it.

00:12:14.160 --> 00:12:18.800
I had the particular skills that were most
needed at that point.

00:12:18.800 --> 00:12:19.870
Financial skills.

00:12:19.870 --> 00:12:24.640
Cuz they needed a financial re-engineering
as well as some leadership skills.

00:12:24.640 --> 00:12:26.540
And so I came back to the consulting firm.

00:12:26.540 --> 00:12:27.090
The Olympics?

00:12:27.090 --> 00:12:28.110
Why go to the Olympics?

00:12:28.110 --> 00:12:30.060
I mean, I I've pointed this out before.

00:12:30.060 --> 00:12:34.630
There was some irony that a person of such
limited athletic talent would be running,

00:12:35.630 --> 00:12:38.790
running the, I mean, I didn't even letter
in a sport in high school, and

00:12:38.790 --> 00:12:42.330
I'd be running the premier sporting event
in the world.

00:12:42.330 --> 00:12:45.356
But I, I and I was not a big fan of the
olympics.

00:12:45.356 --> 00:12:49.707
When I heard that Utah won the games, it's
like, yeah, so what,

00:12:49.707 --> 00:12:55.144
who cares whether and but then, when I got
asked to take a close look at it,

00:12:55.144 --> 00:12:59.010
when it was in trouble, a few things
weighed in my mind.

00:12:59.010 --> 00:13:03.760
One was that this was the community my
parents had been raised in and, and

00:13:03.760 --> 00:13:07.430
it was gonna be tarnished by a potential
scandal.

00:13:07.430 --> 00:13:12.500
Number two, I began to recognize that the
Olympics is one

00:13:12.500 --> 00:13:18.290
of the few remaining places In the world
where, where young people get to see,

00:13:18.290 --> 00:13:21.470
day in and day out, the great qualities of
the human spirit.

00:13:22.630 --> 00:13:24.980
From hard work and

00:13:24.980 --> 00:13:30.390
dedication to patriotism to teamwork,
passion to, to determination.

00:13:30.390 --> 00:13:31.880
I mean the, the list goes on and on.

00:13:31.880 --> 00:13:36.720
We watch the Olympics not because we're
Enthralled with bobsled, you know, or

00:13:36.720 --> 00:13:37.360
ski jumping.

00:13:38.550 --> 00:13:42.840
But instead because we see these young
people from around the world,

00:13:44.300 --> 00:13:48.760
in, in a crucible of stress rising above
it all.

00:13:48.760 --> 00:13:50.990
And great things are, are, are viewed.

00:13:50.990 --> 00:13:53.790
And so I thought it's important these
games go on.

00:13:53.790 --> 00:13:57.630
And I have the particular skillset that's
probably needed at a time like this to get

00:13:57.630 --> 00:14:02.310
the games back on track and the same thing
running for Governor.

00:14:02.310 --> 00:14:06.300
We had a Republican Governor at the time I
was considering coming back after

00:14:06.300 --> 00:14:08.220
the Olympics and running for governor.

00:14:08.220 --> 00:14:14.070
But the approval rating of our Republican
governor was 13% and, and a number of

00:14:14.070 --> 00:14:18.110
republican leaders said we really, would
really appreciate you coming back and

00:14:18.110 --> 00:14:22.280
applying what you've learned in your prior
experiences to help our state turn around.

00:14:22.280 --> 00:14:24.350
We've got this massive, massive deficit.

00:14:24.350 --> 00:14:27.180
And a lot of people are going to lose
jobs,

00:14:27.180 --> 00:14:29.660
we're going to lose our economic edge as a
state.

00:14:29.660 --> 00:14:30.350
Can you come back?

00:14:30.350 --> 00:14:31.680
And that's what, that's what drew me and

00:14:31.680 --> 00:14:34.050
that's part of the way Why end up running
for President?

00:14:34.050 --> 00:14:37.830
It was out of a sense of obligation and,
and love for the country.

00:14:37.830 --> 00:14:40.690
And a sense that I was in the right place
at the right time.

00:14:40.690 --> 00:14:41.500
And with one thing.

00:14:41.500 --> 00:14:42.790
You've heard the quote many times.

00:14:42.790 --> 00:14:45.640
My mother asked it, or said it all the
time.

00:14:45.640 --> 00:14:46.780
If not now, when?

00:14:46.780 --> 00:14:48.230
If not here, where?

00:14:48.230 --> 00:14:50.370
And if not me, who?

00:14:50.370 --> 00:14:53.450
And, and she said if, if you're the right
person at the right time,

00:14:53.450 --> 00:14:54.799
how can you possibly walk away?

00:14:56.332 --> 00:14:59.883
&gt;&gt; I am interested because clearly your
drive comes from this commitment to public

00:14:59.883 --> 00:15:03.116
service, but, you mentioned your father as
a great influence on you and

00:15:03.116 --> 00:15:04.241
you talk about him a lot.

00:15:04.241 --> 00:15:07.121
And one of the quotes from your, the
documentary on you,

00:15:07.121 --> 00:15:11.021
is you talking about him and saying, you
know, you always think about dad and

00:15:11.021 --> 00:15:12.646
how you stand on his shoulders.

00:15:12.646 --> 00:15:17.120
How could you go from his beginnings to I
can run for president?

00:15:17.120 --> 00:15:18.480
I started off where he ended up.

00:15:18.480 --> 00:15:21.010
And that's a very self-reflective
statement that I think is

00:15:21.010 --> 00:15:21.670
really interesting.

00:15:21.670 --> 00:15:25.130
And I'm wondering how much of his success
pushes you to strive higher,

00:15:25.130 --> 00:15:28.810
to aim higher, to push yourself more than
maybe you would have otherwise.

00:15:28.810 --> 00:15:33.920
&gt;&gt; You know, I think some people compete
with their dad or

00:15:33.920 --> 00:15:39.130
their mom because they have a sense that,
that will fulfill who they are or

00:15:39.130 --> 00:15:43.350
define them as a success because they beat
their dad or they beat their mom.

00:15:43.350 --> 00:15:44.880
I'm not in that category.

00:15:44.880 --> 00:15:49.208
I didn't feel in any way I needed to
compete with my dad.

00:15:49.208 --> 00:15:54.966
In part because he was uncompetitive, with
family or friends, or whatever.

00:15:54.966 --> 00:15:57.510
A guy entirely without guile.

00:15:57.510 --> 00:16:02.315
That said, what my dad and my mom taught
us in our home, and

00:16:02.315 --> 00:16:08.130
the way they they lived their life
affected the way I developed.

00:16:08.130 --> 00:16:11.876
And, and led me to have certain skills I
probably wouldn't have had without that

00:16:11.876 --> 00:16:15.789
upbringing, and gave me a perspective on
life I probably wouldn't have had without

00:16:15.789 --> 00:16:17.453
being raised in their home.

00:16:17.453 --> 00:16:22.393
I, I was young, lucky, lucky to be the
youngest in the family, and, and

00:16:22.393 --> 00:16:25.922
I was six years younger than my next
oldest sibling.

00:16:25.922 --> 00:16:30.380
And the result of that was that I was kind
of home with my parents for the last six

00:16:30.380 --> 00:16:35.130
years after my brother went up to school,
and so Dad could take me to work with him.

00:16:35.130 --> 00:16:36.783
And, and I would watch my dad,

00:16:36.783 --> 00:16:41.505
first when I was younger at American
Motors, interacting with executives there.

00:16:41.505 --> 00:16:44.922
Then I saw him as Governor, and would go
to meetings and watch him.

00:16:44.922 --> 00:16:49.686
My summer jobs were working at the
Governor's office, signing his name,

00:16:49.686 --> 00:16:50.462
by the way.

00:16:50.462 --> 00:16:51.048
[LAUGH] On, on, on notary certificates,
all right?

00:16:51.048 --> 00:16:55.382
It's very strange, that I authorized
people's signatures with my dad's false

00:16:55.382 --> 00:16:57.089
signature, but none the less.

00:16:57.089 --> 00:16:59.647
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I, I got, I got to watch,

00:16:59.647 --> 00:17:02.465
I got to watch how my dad interacted with
people,

00:17:02.465 --> 00:17:06.339
not recognizing that I was really learning
from that experience.

00:17:06.339 --> 00:17:11.995
And so, yeah, my, my dad and, and, and to
a great extent also my mom,

00:17:11.995 --> 00:17:15.454
and the commitment they made to family and
church and

00:17:15.454 --> 00:17:20.422
community shaped what I felt was right
about how I should live my life.

00:17:20.422 --> 00:17:22.811
&gt;&gt; I wanna turn to leadership and
management, and

00:17:22.811 --> 00:17:26.589
talk about some of the success stories
you've had of which there are many.

00:17:26.589 --> 00:17:31.876
I'm curious, when you go, you, you've led
multiple different types of organizations,

00:17:31.876 --> 00:17:34.770
private sector, public sector, non-profit.

00:17:34.770 --> 00:17:37.674
When you go into any these situations, are
there any leadership or management

00:17:37.674 --> 00:17:40.672
principles you take with you no matter
where you go, that you found successful?

00:17:40.672 --> 00:17:46.690
&gt;&gt; You know, and, and I mentioned this to
you, Ryan before we came in here.

00:17:46.690 --> 00:17:51.280
And, that is I, I was one day sitting with
a the Chief Financial Officer of my state,

00:17:51.280 --> 00:17:53.360
who was someone I'd hired from Bain
Capital, and

00:17:53.360 --> 00:17:55.620
before that he'd worked at Bain and I had
Bain Consulting.

00:17:55.620 --> 00:17:58.571
So, I knew him well, Eric Kriss is his
name,

00:17:58.571 --> 00:18:01.700
and a nephew of Milton Friedman by the
way.

00:18:01.700 --> 00:18:04.490
But Eric said to me, Mitt, there are two
types of leaders.

00:18:04.490 --> 00:18:07.762
One is the kind of leader that knows a
particular techniques and

00:18:07.762 --> 00:18:09.255
skills they use in leading.

00:18:09.255 --> 00:18:11.714
And the other is someone who has no clue
why they're a leader.

00:18:11.714 --> 00:18:14.255
You're in the latter group [LAUGH].

00:18:14.255 --> 00:18:15.938
So, I'm not sure I'm, I'm gonna be able
to, to help you with this,

00:18:15.938 --> 00:18:16.580
with this question.

00:18:16.580 --> 00:18:21.516
But there are, there is no question in, in
my mind, but the things that,

00:18:21.516 --> 00:18:26.340
that allowed me to be effective in
consulting and

00:18:26.340 --> 00:18:30.200
then In helping form a private equity firm
and venture capital firm.

00:18:30.200 --> 00:18:33.150
And then in the Olympics and then in the
state.

00:18:33.150 --> 00:18:38.250
That, that those kinds of, of attributes
passed from one one sphere to the next.

00:18:39.420 --> 00:18:40.940
I, I don't know what they all are.

00:18:40.940 --> 00:18:45.810
One, one is, I believe, having a clear
vision of where you want to go and, and

00:18:45.810 --> 00:18:48.350
being able to articulate that to yourself.

00:18:48.350 --> 00:18:49.380
Perhaps writing it down.

00:18:49.380 --> 00:18:53.310
I tend to, I take out the, the notes
section of my iPad and, and will,

00:18:53.310 --> 00:18:57.200
will write down what it is I want to
accomplish and

00:18:57.200 --> 00:19:01.000
think about precisely what I hope to
accomplish with a particular assignment.

00:19:01.000 --> 00:19:02.898
So, I'll write that down.

00:19:02.898 --> 00:19:05.730
So, having a clear objective, I think, is
important.

00:19:05.730 --> 00:19:09.220
Number two I think it's important to know
what your values are.

00:19:09.220 --> 00:19:10.320
I didn't ever write that down.

00:19:10.320 --> 00:19:11.990
I just knew what they were.

00:19:11.990 --> 00:19:15.960
But, for me, people and, and associations
with other people and

00:19:15.960 --> 00:19:18.860
friendships with people were more
important than,

00:19:18.860 --> 00:19:22.120
than any other aspect of what an
enterprise might be doing.

00:19:22.120 --> 00:19:25.110
More important than profit, more important
than gaining market share,

00:19:25.110 --> 00:19:26.990
more important than promotion.

00:19:26.990 --> 00:19:30.800
What, what, were the feelings I had for
other people and they had for me.

00:19:30.800 --> 00:19:35.610
And so I have, I don't try and treat
people with respect,

00:19:35.610 --> 00:19:38.840
I do treat people with respect because I
respect people.

00:19:38.840 --> 00:19:41.530
It's not that I say to myself to be an
effective leader I have to

00:19:41.530 --> 00:19:42.780
be respectful of people.

00:19:42.780 --> 00:19:44.800
No, I mean, I am respectful of people,

00:19:46.180 --> 00:19:51.840
because I respect other human beings as
being sons and daughters of God.

00:19:51.840 --> 00:19:54.730
And, and equal in all respects to me.

00:19:54.730 --> 00:19:57.950
So, that, that is, I think that's just
part of, kind of, I mean,

00:19:57.950 --> 00:19:59.180
again I get that from my parents.

00:19:59.180 --> 00:20:02.420
That's just part of who you are, or who
you aren't, as the case may be.

00:20:02.420 --> 00:20:03.754
I don't think you can fake that.

00:20:03.754 --> 00:20:08.880
And, and I, I'll mention one other thing,
and then you, you can come back to it.

00:20:08.880 --> 00:20:11.339
I like a, or push me further if you'd
like.

00:20:11.339 --> 00:20:14.380
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; As a management style,

00:20:14.380 --> 00:20:16.600
I like extensive participation.

00:20:16.600 --> 00:20:18.780
I like a lot of give and take.

00:20:18.780 --> 00:20:21.740
I remember on one occasion

00:20:21.740 --> 00:20:24.490
the Chief of Staff while I was serving as
Governor came in.

00:20:24.490 --> 00:20:25.185
And she said look,

00:20:25.185 --> 00:20:28.530
we've had this tough decision to make
about whether to spend, I can't remember

00:20:28.530 --> 00:20:32.130
how many hundreds of million of dollars
putting in this new subway line.

00:20:32.130 --> 00:20:33.820
And and, and she said,

00:20:33.820 --> 00:20:38.500
I'm going to bring in the cabinet members,
we all agreed, that we should do it.

00:20:38.500 --> 00:20:42.240
All of us admit, we've gone through the
numbers, and, and the pros and cons, and

00:20:42.240 --> 00:20:43.270
we all agree we should do it.

00:20:43.270 --> 00:20:47.170
And they all sat there, and I said, does
anyone here disagree with this decision?

00:20:47.170 --> 00:20:48.990
And they said no that they were all on
board, and

00:20:48.990 --> 00:20:50.650
I said then I can't possibly go ahead.

00:20:50.650 --> 00:20:51.820
He said, what do you mean?

00:20:51.820 --> 00:20:54.490
I said, well, I have to have someone here
who disagrees.

00:20:54.490 --> 00:20:58.710
We've gotta have someone who can make an
argument for why it's a bad decision.

00:20:58.710 --> 00:21:01.920
Pl, please go back and find, if you have
to, someone on the other side of

00:21:01.920 --> 00:21:05.220
the aisle, in the Senate or the House, or
someone who really disagrees with this,

00:21:05.220 --> 00:21:08.130
and then let's take it apart once we can
have that kind of debate.

00:21:08.130 --> 00:21:11.919
I love that kind of exchange and debate.

00:21:11.919 --> 00:21:15.350
And, and, not because, you know,

00:21:15.350 --> 00:21:18.610
I think I'm the judge who can always pick
out the right, the right answer, but

00:21:18.610 --> 00:21:21.590
because I enjoy the give and take, and the
mental exchange.

00:21:21.590 --> 00:21:24.290
And I find when you have that kind of
exchange,

00:21:24.290 --> 00:21:26.616
often times I end up being wrong.

00:21:26.616 --> 00:21:29.340
Often times others are wrong, but we learn
from it.

00:21:29.340 --> 00:21:34.130
And I am entirely non-defensive about
whether my answer, the one,

00:21:34.130 --> 00:21:37.360
the preconceived notion I came in with was
right or not.

00:21:37.360 --> 00:21:38.800
I only care about getting to the right
answer.

00:21:38.800 --> 00:21:41.180
And if the group, if as a group we can
come up with a right answer,

00:21:41.180 --> 00:21:43.750
I don't care whose idea it was.

00:21:43.750 --> 00:21:48.230
It's like yeah, let's, let, you know, it's
the fun and the engagement of drawing on

00:21:48.230 --> 00:21:51.760
people and their experience that I, that I
find compelling.

00:21:51.760 --> 00:21:53.650
And I'll mention one more thing.

00:21:53.650 --> 00:21:58.470
And that is, I, I used to think I should
spend my time,

00:21:58.470 --> 00:22:03.700
as a leader, working with people to help
them overcome their weaknesses.

00:22:03.700 --> 00:22:06.090
And then I realized that's a waste of
time, because, by and large,

00:22:06.090 --> 00:22:08.410
people don't overcome their weaknesses.

00:22:08.410 --> 00:22:11.909
Instead, I found that my job was to help
people take advantage of their strengths.

00:22:13.050 --> 00:22:17.690
And if they had weaknesses, define ways to
ac, to accommodate that by bringing in

00:22:17.690 --> 00:22:20.690
other people who had the strengths where
they had weaknesses.

00:22:20.690 --> 00:22:22.160
Or where I had weaknesses.

00:22:22.160 --> 00:22:23.570
And I do, by the way.

00:22:23.570 --> 00:22:25.900
And by the way, I have tended to bring in
people who,

00:22:25.900 --> 00:22:28.748
who can complement my own weaknesses.

00:22:28.748 --> 00:22:30.720
And, and so it being capital, for

00:22:30.720 --> 00:22:34.460
instance, I, we are a, a very different
group of people.

00:22:34.460 --> 00:22:38.340
It's not th, th, th, the 18 partners that
were there when I left.

00:22:38.340 --> 00:22:39.560
Now there are over 100.

00:22:39.560 --> 00:22:44.620
But the 18 partners who were there had
very different personalities and skills.

00:22:44.620 --> 00:22:48.880
In part because I saw my job as not trying
to make them all the same, but

00:22:48.880 --> 00:22:51.890
instead taking advantage of the particular
skills people had and

00:22:51.890 --> 00:22:56.099
encouraging those things and filling in
th, the blank spots and the flat spots.

00:22:57.320 --> 00:23:05.490
Where where they may not have been, may
not have been quite as effective.

00:23:05.490 --> 00:23:10.520
&gt;&gt; Your most recent management challenge
was your presidential organization.

00:23:10.520 --> 00:23:11.090
And I'm curious.

00:23:11.090 --> 00:23:11.590
&gt;&gt; You may heard. I didn't win. [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:23:11.590 --> 00:23:12.090
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:23:12.090 --> 00:23:12.673
&gt;&gt; I did hear that.

00:23:12.673 --> 00:23:13.213
And and, and, and

00:23:13.213 --> 00:23:14.813
that's actually what I wanna ask about,

00:23:14.813 --> 00:23:17.970
is when you look back at managing that
organization.

00:23:17.970 --> 00:23:20.170
I know everyone can play Monday morning
quarterback in politics,

00:23:20.170 --> 00:23:23.400
but when you look back at managing that
organization,

00:23:23.400 --> 00:23:25.660
what was different about managing a
presidential campaign?

00:23:26.830 --> 00:23:28.860
And, and maybe what would you have done
differently if you,

00:23:28.860 --> 00:23:31.020
if you looked at it from a management
standpoint again?

00:23:32.460 --> 00:23:37.390
&gt;&gt; Well, a candidate is by and large not
the manager of the campaign.

00:23:37.390 --> 00:23:42.440
And, and and that has brought home to you
day in and

00:23:42.440 --> 00:23:44.807
day out by the people who are managing the
campaign.

00:23:44.807 --> 00:23:47.712
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And, and and so I you know I,

00:23:47.712 --> 00:23:49.666
I have run for office before.

00:23:49.666 --> 00:23:52.730
I've worked on my mother's campaign for US
Senate.

00:23:52.730 --> 00:23:54.959
I worked on my father's campaigns.

00:23:54.959 --> 00:23:58.209
My father ran for governor three times and
was elected three times.

00:23:58.209 --> 00:24:00.030
He ran for president, was not elected.

00:24:00.030 --> 00:24:02.916
But I worked on my dad's campaigns, my
mother's campaign and

00:24:02.916 --> 00:24:04.990
the candidate is really out there
speaking.

00:24:04.990 --> 00:24:09.400
The candidate is out, is out doing the job
of connecting with people and,

00:24:09.400 --> 00:24:14.330
and and, and taking the message to the, to
the voting public.

00:24:14.330 --> 00:24:18.180
The people running the campaign you choose
like you would if you were the,

00:24:18.180 --> 00:24:19.720
the chairman of the board.

00:24:19.720 --> 00:24:22.020
And you choose someone to be the chief
executive officer,

00:24:22.020 --> 00:24:23.140
the chief operating officer.

00:24:23.140 --> 00:24:24.390
And you have both in a campaign.

00:24:24.390 --> 00:24:28.200
You have a CEO and a COO in a well run
campaign.

00:24:28.200 --> 00:24:31.050
And so I was not the CEO or the COO of my
campaign.

00:24:31.050 --> 00:24:34.640
I was probably more like the chairman of
the, of the campaign.

00:24:34.640 --> 00:24:38.590
And, and key strategic decisions, I
insisted on being a part of.

00:24:38.590 --> 00:24:42.980
And, and managed in the way that, that
I've just described.

00:24:42.980 --> 00:24:45.070
We would have probably the eight or

00:24:45.070 --> 00:24:50.500
ten top people in the campaign come
together and debate major issues.

00:24:50.500 --> 00:24:52.655
So if, in a presidential campaign.

00:24:52.655 --> 00:24:54.495
Was I gonna play in Iowa?

00:24:54.495 --> 00:24:58.365
This is in 2012, I played in 2008 and, and

00:24:58.365 --> 00:25:02.055
found Iowa to be, Iowa to be expensive and
a good start but not sufficient.

00:25:02.055 --> 00:25:03.371
Was I gonna play in Iowa or

00:25:03.371 --> 00:25:07.190
was it hopeless and should I just go
directly to New Hampshire?

00:25:07.190 --> 00:25:08.970
And we debated that at great length.

00:25:08.970 --> 00:25:13.220
And and talked about a strategy to go
forward, and, and, and

00:25:13.220 --> 00:25:15.140
selected a strategy which worked very
well.

00:25:15.140 --> 00:25:18.310
Winning the nomination, by the way, is not
easy.

00:25:18.310 --> 00:25:21.250
You know after it's over it all looks very
easy.

00:25:21.250 --> 00:25:22.960
Oh you were obviously gonna get the
nomination.

00:25:22.960 --> 00:25:26.960
Oh yeah, I mean, I was behind Rick
Santorum, in the last three states.

00:25:26.960 --> 00:25:28.410
I believe Iowa.

00:25:28.410 --> 00:25:29.080
I don't mean Iowa.

00:25:29.080 --> 00:25:30.670
Illinois.

00:25:30.670 --> 00:25:33.250
Wisconsin, Michigan, also, Ohio.

00:25:33.250 --> 00:25:35.770
I think I was be, behind five or ten
points,

00:25:35.770 --> 00:25:39.200
with only days to go before the the
primary.

00:25:39.200 --> 00:25:43.080
And so, it is tough to do that, and I
think we did pretty, pretty well, I think,

00:25:43.080 --> 00:25:44.029
in the general election.

00:25:45.150 --> 00:25:48.440
We faced some real challenges and made
some big mistakes.

00:25:48.440 --> 00:25:53.050
And, and, but as I looked back at the, at
the process that we pursued,

00:25:53.050 --> 00:25:56.210
I'm, I'm pleased with the process and
pleased with the people.

00:25:56.210 --> 00:25:58.080
We had a really good team of people.

00:25:58.080 --> 00:25:59.970
Very committed and one of the things I
liked about it,

00:25:59.970 --> 00:26:04.610
there was the not the kind of politics
inside the organization you sometimes see.

00:26:04.610 --> 00:26:08.530
People, you know, backstabbing and trying
to claim credit for good things.

00:26:09.834 --> 00:26:12.100
You know, people have strengths and
weaknesses.

00:26:12.100 --> 00:26:16.020
Each of our team members, had like myself,
had real weaknesses, but

00:26:16.020 --> 00:26:21.730
we worked well by and large and, and got a
lot of things right and some things wrong.

00:26:21.730 --> 00:26:23.550
You mentioned things wrong.

00:26:23.550 --> 00:26:29.280
One of the big challenges that a
Republican candidate has is that,

00:26:29.280 --> 00:26:34.090
that minority voters tend not to vote
extensively in Republican primaries,

00:26:35.140 --> 00:26:36.980
or participate in Republican caucuses.

00:26:36.980 --> 00:26:40.390
They tend to vote in democratic primaries
and democratic caucuses.

00:26:40.390 --> 00:26:43.440
So if you're Jeb Bush right now or Chris
Christie or Marco Rubio or

00:26:43.440 --> 00:26:46.420
Scott Walker and you want to get the
nomination,

00:26:46.420 --> 00:26:49.610
you're going to be going to the people who
vote in your primaries and

00:26:49.610 --> 00:26:54.300
in your caucuses which will, by and large,
not be minority voters.

00:26:54.300 --> 00:26:57.370
So for the next year you're gonna watch
the candidates on my side of

00:26:57.370 --> 00:27:01.190
the aisle spend all their time with the
white population and

00:27:01.190 --> 00:27:03.610
typically not at colleges either.

00:27:03.610 --> 00:27:06.410
And then when they get the nomination,
when somebody finally becomes the nominee,

00:27:06.410 --> 00:27:07.870
they run to the minority community and

00:27:07.870 --> 00:27:10.450
say give me your vote and they say where
have you been?

00:27:10.450 --> 00:27:11.290
All right?

00:27:11.290 --> 00:27:12.870
And that was a mistake I made.

00:27:12.870 --> 00:27:15.300
Which is, I was so anxious to get the
nomination,

00:27:15.300 --> 00:27:20.280
I didn't spend as much time as I should
have taking my message to minority voters,

00:27:20.280 --> 00:27:24.080
fighting on minority, or in this case,
Hispanic T, uh,TV and

00:27:24.080 --> 00:27:27.930
radio airwaves, getting my message across,
even though it wouldn't have helped me in

00:27:27.930 --> 00:27:31.810
the primary necessarily It certainly was
essential in the general.

00:27:31.810 --> 00:27:35.950
And, I think that was something we missed
in our strategy sessions.

00:27:35.950 --> 00:27:38.800
And, in part because we looked at what had
happened in the past.

00:27:38.800 --> 00:27:42.540
We said we had to target independent
voters so we went after independence.

00:27:42.540 --> 00:27:44.420
I won independent voters.

00:27:44.420 --> 00:27:47.240
Won them in, in, in Ohio among other
states.

00:27:47.240 --> 00:27:50.380
We said hey, if you got it, independent
vote in Ohio, you are gonna win.

00:27:50.380 --> 00:27:54.934
No they're not [LAUGH] we needed a much
better showing among minority voters and

00:27:54.934 --> 00:27:57.901
that's a place we really messed up and you
know, so

00:27:57.901 --> 00:28:00.604
I go back and say would I have changed the
team?

00:28:00.604 --> 00:28:03.160
I, I like the team, I, I was proud of the
team we had.

00:28:03.160 --> 00:28:04.580
And the way we work together.

00:28:04.580 --> 00:28:06.639
But do we make a mistake in strategy?

00:28:06.639 --> 00:28:08.477
Sure, that, that among others.

00:28:08.477 --> 00:28:11.270
&gt;&gt; Moving to the idea of running for
office.

00:28:11.270 --> 00:28:14.760
People in the room have come up to me and
said they want to pursue a career

00:28:14.760 --> 00:28:18.000
in business like you, and then run for
office, like you.

00:28:18.000 --> 00:28:18.500
I'm curious.

00:28:19.580 --> 00:28:21.260
If you were to give advice today to
someone like that,

00:28:21.260 --> 00:28:26.470
would you follow your father's advice,
which was, you know, find a career, find

00:28:26.470 --> 00:28:30.760
a reason not to run, and then run later on
when you don't have so much at stake?

00:28:30.760 --> 00:28:33.340
Or would it be something different in
today's age?

00:28:34.500 --> 00:28:36.885
&gt;&gt; I'm afraid I'm, I'm prisoner of my
dad's advice.

00:28:36.885 --> 00:28:39.220
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And, and, and I'll tell you why.

00:28:39.220 --> 00:28:43.970
My dad's advice was, because my dad ran
for governor when he was 56 years old.

00:28:43.970 --> 00:28:45.856
He'd been head of a car company.

00:28:45.856 --> 00:28:50.350
He he felt that his state was circling the
drain, the state of Michigan.

00:28:50.350 --> 00:28:54.590
He saw that, that automobile jobs were
leaving, his state, going to other states,

00:28:54.590 --> 00:29:00.176
and, and other countries, and felt that he
had to try and turn things around.

00:29:00.176 --> 00:29:03.484
He felt that race relations in the state
were a word, awful and

00:29:03.484 --> 00:29:06.100
civil rights have not been advanced as
they should.

00:29:06.100 --> 00:29:10.950
That the schools and the city, Detroit,
were, were just a tragedy.

00:29:10.950 --> 00:29:13.342
And so, he got involved and ran for
governor.

00:29:13.342 --> 00:29:15.610
I got involved in his campaigns.

00:29:15.610 --> 00:29:18.790
I found politics very exciting, and he
said to me,

00:29:18.790 --> 00:29:21.070
as also to my sisters and brother.

00:29:21.070 --> 00:29:25.950
He said, you know, I wouldn't get involved
in politics until your kids are raised and

00:29:25.950 --> 00:29:27.780
if, and only if you're financially
independent.

00:29:28.780 --> 00:29:30.060
And it's like wow.

00:29:30.060 --> 00:29:31.610
That, that's never gonna happen.

00:29:31.610 --> 00:29:35.860
I thought I mean, I mean you know working
in, in consulting is great but

00:29:35.860 --> 00:29:38.090
it's not gonna make you financially
independent.

00:29:40.410 --> 00:29:43.340
And so I never imagined I'd get involved
in politics.

00:29:43.340 --> 00:29:46.362
And and what happened was starting Bain
Capital and

00:29:46.362 --> 00:29:50.155
then having the stock market take off and,
if you're leveraged, and

00:29:50.155 --> 00:29:54.997
the stock market goes from 1,000 to
10,000, it's a good thing, all right?

00:29:54.997 --> 00:29:55.497
And so.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:29:55.497 --> 00:29:59.385
&gt;&gt; So suddenly I bec, I, those, those

00:29:59.385 --> 00:30:03.151
conditions became met. I, I. &gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; That's the euphemism of the day.

00:30:03.151 --> 00:30:03.711
&gt;&gt; I'll write that

00:30:03.711 --> 00:30:04.383
down, yeah.
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:30:04.383 --> 00:30:04.908
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:30:04.908 --> 00:30:06.287
&gt;&gt; But I, I personally, and

00:30:06.287 --> 00:30:10.702
I don't, I don't think you have to be
financially independent to run for office.

00:30:10.702 --> 00:30:12.343
I, I think you have to be able to,

00:30:12.343 --> 00:30:15.221
to meet your mortgage without having to
win the election,

00:30:15.221 --> 00:30:15.943
however.
And

00:30:15.943 --> 00:30:20.714
that was what my dad was concerned about.
I I think it really helps,

00:30:20.714 --> 00:30:24.278
if people who.
Who go to work in the statehouse or

00:30:24.278 --> 00:30:29.670
go to work in Washington actually have
experience in the real economy and

00:30:29.670 --> 00:30:32.720
in the real world and can take that
experience to government.

00:30:32.720 --> 00:30:38.160
Whether that's in teaching, or working in
the foreign service, or, or working,

00:30:38.160 --> 00:30:43.120
in a corporation, I think it really helps
yo go there with background and

00:30:43.120 --> 00:30:45.080
experience that you can share with others.

00:30:45.080 --> 00:30:47.590
I think that was the concept with the,

00:30:48.590 --> 00:30:51.170
that the founders had in mind in forming
our, our Republic.

00:30:51.170 --> 00:30:55.310
And that is that you had come from a, a
real, I mean look at John Adams.

00:30:55.310 --> 00:30:58.400
I mean, he was a, a farmer in
Massachusetts, went to serve,

00:30:58.400 --> 00:31:02.500
became President, went backed home and,
and went back home, became a farmer again.

00:31:02.500 --> 00:31:06.330
And, and I think that's a better model
then what we have right now.

00:31:06.330 --> 00:31:08.850
And so, you know, my advice would be.

00:31:08.850 --> 00:31:10.100
Yeah, work at a real job.

00:31:10.100 --> 00:31:11.330
Get some experience.

00:31:11.330 --> 00:31:14.800
And if the window opens, you see an
opportunity to serve, locally,

00:31:14.800 --> 00:31:17.080
state wide, nationally.

00:31:17.080 --> 00:31:18.090
Then jump in.

00:31:18.090 --> 00:31:19.609
Because good people are needed badly.

00:31:20.620 --> 00:31:24.747
You, another quote you, you said in your
documentary was that the other

00:31:24.747 --> 00:31:28.570
side often acts like they don't know what
it's like to own a business.

00:31:28.570 --> 00:31:32.110
They don't know what it's like to have
everything on the line.

00:31:32.110 --> 00:31:34.260
And I see that disconnect a lot today,

00:31:34.260 --> 00:31:36.850
especially with income inequality as a
burgeoning issue.

00:31:38.070 --> 00:31:41.560
In particular, private equity is something
that you were tremendously successful in,

00:31:41.560 --> 00:31:43.870
in the campaign talked about it in a
different way.

00:31:43.870 --> 00:31:47.068
And a lot of our classmates are going into
private equity or

00:31:47.068 --> 00:31:51.245
going into these business careers that are
seen as very prestigious here but

00:31:51.245 --> 00:31:53.480
maybe elsewhere are seen differently.

00:31:53.480 --> 00:31:56.302
I'm wondering what you think about that
disconnect and

00:31:56.302 --> 00:32:00.605
whether there's ways we can bridge that
divide or how you think about it now.

00:32:00.605 --> 00:32:06.009
Well a couple of things, one, my, my
concern about incomes

00:32:06.009 --> 00:32:10.726
in America is not so much focused on why
some people make so

00:32:10.726 --> 00:32:15.770
much, as opposed to why are so many people
making so little.

00:32:15.770 --> 00:32:20.450
And, and we have large cohorts of people
in this country

00:32:20.450 --> 00:32:24.500
who have not seen rising incomes, and and
have not seen it for a long time.

00:32:25.550 --> 00:32:29.990
And, and so my concern is how do we get
people out of generational poverty?

00:32:29.990 --> 00:32:31.980
How do we get people out of situational
poverty?

00:32:31.980 --> 00:32:35.940
And how do we get the whole middle class
in America, as well as lower income folks,

00:32:35.940 --> 00:32:37.960
to see rising incomes again?

00:32:37.960 --> 00:32:39.890
And that, that for me is the big, is the
big issue.

00:32:40.940 --> 00:32:44.520
I, I don't look at peop, at Steve Jobs and
say boy, he's a bad person for

00:32:44.520 --> 00:32:45.660
making all that money.

00:32:45.660 --> 00:32:49.790
I, I look and say thank heavens for Steve
Jobs, Bill Gates and, and

00:32:49.790 --> 00:32:53.110
Scott McNealy and, and Marc Andreessen,
more power to them.

00:32:53.110 --> 00:32:55.040
Do, be more and more successful.

00:32:55.040 --> 00:32:58.130
And, by the way, if you wanna give it all
away during life, that's fine too.

00:32:58.130 --> 00:32:59.030
Great.

00:32:59.030 --> 00:33:03.430
But, so I don't look and say okay those, I
don't think those people are the problem,

00:33:03.430 --> 00:33:06.230
I think the problem is how come we're not
able to lift more?

00:33:06.230 --> 00:33:11.094
How come we can't get more people to see
rising incomes and, and more prosperity?

00:33:11.094 --> 00:33:15.883
And there are a lot of reasons for, for
that, but, but I happen to believe very

00:33:15.883 --> 00:33:20.881
fundamentally and profoundly, that without
question, that the best principles for

00:33:20.881 --> 00:33:25.124
helping people get out of poverty and
seeing rising incomes is for, is for

00:33:25.124 --> 00:33:28.700
us to follow conservative and Republican
principles.

00:33:28.700 --> 00:33:31.100
Now, you might say oh well that's, that
doesn't make sense,

00:33:31.100 --> 00:33:32.730
you're just the party of the rich.

00:33:32.730 --> 00:33:37.460
Let me tell you friends, the rich will do
fine whether Democrats are in power,

00:33:37.460 --> 00:33:39.660
liberals are in power, conservatives or
Republicans.

00:33:39.660 --> 00:33:41.560
The rich do fine all over the world.

00:33:41.560 --> 00:33:42.440
The rich do just fine.

00:33:43.460 --> 00:33:46.716
The people whose lives are affected by
politics and

00:33:46.716 --> 00:33:51.344
leadership are the people in the middle
and the people at the bottom end.

00:33:51.344 --> 00:33:55.488
And the reason I'm a Republican is that I
believe the principles that,

00:33:55.488 --> 00:33:58.677
that my party stands for, or at least that
I stand for.

00:33:58.677 --> 00:34:03.276
That those principles are the best
designed to help people see rising incomes

00:34:03.276 --> 00:34:06.120
again and better jobs for their kids.

00:34:06.120 --> 00:34:09.135
And, and the best principles to get people
out of poverty.

00:34:09.135 --> 00:34:11.191
I, I mean I, don't, I mean I,

00:34:11.191 --> 00:34:16.214
and we could have this debate at great
length but I think evidence proves it.

00:34:16.214 --> 00:34:17.140
I think logic proves it.

00:34:17.140 --> 00:34:18.090
That's why I'm Republican.

00:34:18.090 --> 00:34:21.120
That's why I ran for office is I want to
help people in the middle and

00:34:21.120 --> 00:34:23.530
in the bottom see rising incomes and, and

00:34:23.530 --> 00:34:26.850
I believe that you're successful, then
you're enterprising.

00:34:26.850 --> 00:34:28.450
You're going into business.

00:34:28.450 --> 00:34:29.960
More power to you.

00:34:29.960 --> 00:34:31.790
Go out there, be successful.

00:34:31.790 --> 00:34:35.800
Build a business, work in a business
that's already there, make it better.

00:34:35.800 --> 00:34:39.660
The better an enterprise does, the more
profits it makes, the more you can invest.

00:34:39.660 --> 00:34:41.280
The more you invest, the more you can
grow.

00:34:41.280 --> 00:34:43.860
The more you grow, the more people you can
hire.

00:34:43.860 --> 00:34:47.130
The more people that are being hired, the
more wages will go up.

00:34:47.130 --> 00:34:52.780
Wages go up because there's demand for
labor that outstrips the supply of labor.

00:34:52.780 --> 00:34:56.830
That's how they go up, and so, I, I want
you to succeed.

00:34:56.830 --> 00:35:00.690
I mean, I look at you and when you're all
highly successful businesspeople as

00:35:00.690 --> 00:35:04.094
you hope you will be, I won't look at you
and say there's the enemy.

00:35:04.094 --> 00:35:08.801
I'll say they're my friends, I love you, I
appreciate what you're doing,

00:35:08.801 --> 00:35:10.594
I want to see you successful.

00:35:10.594 --> 00:35:15.518
And as you're successful, I want you to
recognize that your success is

00:35:15.518 --> 00:35:18.720
contributing to the success of our country
and

00:35:18.720 --> 00:35:22.677
to those people who rely on the jobs
you'll help create.

00:35:22.677 --> 00:35:23.841
&gt;&gt; We only have time for

00:35:23.841 --> 00:35:27.704
one more question before we're gonna move
to audience Q and A.

00:35:27.704 --> 00:35:29.510
And it's a simple question, maybe.

00:35:29.510 --> 00:35:31.510
It's just what does the future hold for
you?

00:35:31.510 --> 00:35:33.469
You're boxing Evander Holyfield.

00:35:33.469 --> 00:35:34.010
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:35:34.010 --> 00:35:34.510
Yeah.
&gt;&gt; In the near future.

00:35:34.510 --> 00:35:35.135
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:35:35.135 --> 00:35:35.635
&gt;&gt; Which

00:35:35.635 --> 00:35:36.969
&gt;&gt; Is a risky.

00:35:36.969 --> 00:35:38.826
I can say is a risky decision to me,

00:35:38.826 --> 00:35:41.404
but you know, I
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] The term pulling his punches

00:35:41.404 --> 00:35:42.219
come to mind, yeah.

00:35:42.219 --> 00:35:42.885
&gt;&gt; Yeah, hopefully.

00:35:42.885 --> 00:35:43.385
Yeah.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Yeah.

00:35:43.385 --> 00:35:44.213
Yeah.

00:35:44.213 --> 00:35:47.514
&gt;&gt; But I'm curious, you know, what, what
do you want to do next,

00:35:47.514 --> 00:35:49.400
given all you've accomplished?

00:35:49.400 --> 00:35:54.100
&gt;&gt; Well, you know, I, I would, I continue,
I continue to be motivated

00:35:54.100 --> 00:35:58.940
by the same things that got me into
presidential politics in the first place.

00:35:58.940 --> 00:36:01.580
And so I will work for

00:36:01.580 --> 00:36:06.620
individuals who I believe will get America
on the right track to once

00:36:06.620 --> 00:36:11.810
again create more jobs than we have supply
of labor, to see rising incomes again.

00:36:11.810 --> 00:36:14.190
That means better schools, that means
better innovation,

00:36:14.190 --> 00:36:17.420
that means making America the most
attractive place for entrepreneurs and

00:36:17.420 --> 00:36:20.180
innovators and businesses of all kinds.

00:36:20.180 --> 00:36:22.950
It also means an entirely different
foreign policy.

00:36:22.950 --> 00:36:26.360
I happen to think that the foreign policy
of the last six or

00:36:26.360 --> 00:36:31.560
seven years has been a disaster and the
globe is feeling it.

00:36:31.560 --> 00:36:35.600
So I wanna change direction for our
country in, in a way that I think would be

00:36:35.600 --> 00:36:38.780
more productive for the safety of the
world, and the well-being of the world,

00:36:38.780 --> 00:36:42.520
for the preservation of liberty, and for
rising prosperity for all Americans.

00:36:42.520 --> 00:36:43.649
So that how do I do that?

00:36:44.910 --> 00:36:48.308
By campaigning and raising money for
Senate and Congressional seats at the,

00:36:48.308 --> 00:36:49.602
at the national level.

00:36:49.602 --> 00:36:52.401
By helping Republican candidates for
President.

00:36:52.401 --> 00:36:55.503
I'd help the Democrat candidates, but
they, they're not asking for

00:36:55.503 --> 00:36:56.594
my advice at this point.

00:36:56.594 --> 00:36:58.992
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].
&gt;&gt; And there's only one, really,

00:36:58.992 --> 00:36:59.919
at this stage.

00:36:59.919 --> 00:37:02.579
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And, and but I, you know,

00:37:02.579 --> 00:37:08.110
I speak regularly with with people who are
running for office and offer my advice and

00:37:08.110 --> 00:37:13.219
will continue to do so and in, I mean I do
a lot of fundraisers and, and speeches.

00:37:13.219 --> 00:37:15.720
And, by the way the reason I'm here.

00:37:15.720 --> 00:37:18.940
During my campaign, I wasn't at college
campuses and

00:37:18.940 --> 00:37:20.130
business school and law schools.

00:37:20.130 --> 00:37:21.690
I was at Harvard law school last week and

00:37:21.690 --> 00:37:25.520
I was at Duke and, and, I'll be at
University of Chicago coming soon.

00:37:25.520 --> 00:37:28.770
The end of this week I'll be at, at, at,
in Jacksonville, Florida.

00:37:28.770 --> 00:37:32.250
I speak to college campuses cuz I want
people to understand.

00:37:32.250 --> 00:37:33.760
The people who are conservative,

00:37:33.760 --> 00:37:37.970
are conservative, because we believe the
principals of conservatism

00:37:37.970 --> 00:37:40.710
are best able to help the middle class and
the poor.

00:37:40.710 --> 00:37:42.360
We know how to end poverty.

00:37:42.360 --> 00:37:44.240
Generational poverty.

00:37:44.240 --> 00:37:46.070
But we're not doing it.

00:37:46.070 --> 00:37:48.330
Because there's not a political will for
it to happen.

00:37:48.330 --> 00:37:50.427
But, so, that, that's what I'm devoted to.

00:37:50.427 --> 00:37:52.623
Now at the same time I'm back in business,

00:37:52.623 --> 00:37:55.612
because I want to make sure that I can
provide for myself and

00:37:55.612 --> 00:37:59.700
my family without drawing down their
inheritance and and of course e,

00:37:59.700 --> 00:38:04.384
every politician who leaves office says
I'm gonna spend more time with the family.

00:38:04.384 --> 00:38:05.469
I actually am.

00:38:05.469 --> 00:38:08.927
I have 23 grandkids and I'm spending time
with the family and love it.

00:38:08.927 --> 00:38:09.677
Thank you Ryan.

00:38:09.677 --> 00:38:12.180
&gt;&gt; Great.
Well we're gonna move to some Q and A now.

00:38:12.180 --> 00:38:16.230
And there'll be mics on the left and the
right side.

00:38:16.230 --> 00:38:20.000
So raise your hand high if you're
interested in asking a question.

00:38:20.000 --> 00:38:23.052
For those at the top please continue to
Tweet your questions.

00:38:23.052 --> 00:38:25.530
And we're gonna start with a question from
Twitter so

00:38:25.530 --> 00:38:27.135
I'll point to [UNKNOWN] over here.

00:38:27.135 --> 00:38:28.094
Yeah?

00:38:28.094 --> 00:38:32.652
&gt;&gt; What worries you most about the U.S.
economy?

00:38:32.652 --> 00:38:37.126
&gt;&gt; Well I'm, I'm concerned that we may be
seeing a bubble in,

00:38:37.126 --> 00:38:42.472
in in tech stocks and, and that we could
have the, a kind of

00:38:42.472 --> 00:38:48.210
disruptive event that could cause the, the
stock market to collapse.

00:38:48.210 --> 00:38:51.500
And I'm not, I'm not terrified about
people losing money in the stock market.

00:38:51.500 --> 00:38:54.630
I'm terrified about, about going into a
recession again.

00:38:54.630 --> 00:38:56.630
That would put a lot of people out of
work.

00:38:56.630 --> 00:39:01.520
Obviously the Fed has used every tool it
has to get this economy going.

00:39:01.520 --> 00:39:05.000
And if we went into a recession at this
point, there are not a lot of tools.

00:39:05.000 --> 00:39:07.500
I mean we're already at zero, effectively
zero interest rates.

00:39:07.500 --> 00:39:08.260
So there are not a lot of tools.

00:39:08.260 --> 00:39:11.177
We've, we're spending massively more than
we take in.

00:39:11.177 --> 00:39:14.432
So we're, we're pursuing a highly
stimulative monetary policy and

00:39:14.432 --> 00:39:16.528
a highly stimulative fiscal policy both,
so

00:39:16.528 --> 00:39:20.010
if we went into recession at this point
that'd be pretty tough.

00:39:20.010 --> 00:39:21.890
I hope that doesn't happen.

00:39:21.890 --> 00:39:26.700
I think there's some positive, elements on
the, on the horizon that, one of course,

00:39:26.700 --> 00:39:29.620
is very low cost of energy which is
stimulative.

00:39:29.620 --> 00:39:32.120
Gives people more money to spend and those
things are encouraging.

00:39:32.120 --> 00:39:35.430
I don't think we're gonna see something of
an immediate nature that

00:39:35.430 --> 00:39:37.050
causes us to fall into recession again.

00:39:37.050 --> 00:39:40.240
I sure hope not, but the rest of the world
is a little iffy and

00:39:40.240 --> 00:39:41.870
it can have an impact in America.

00:39:41.870 --> 00:39:43.460
Longer term though is my, is my concern.

00:39:43.460 --> 00:39:49.510
Longer term, I see America as adopting of
the policies of Europe.

00:39:49.510 --> 00:39:53.238
Higher and higher corporate taxes,
although Europe is getting away from that.

00:39:53.238 --> 00:39:55.374
I'll say the traditional policies of
Europe.

00:39:55.374 --> 00:39:57.139
Higher and higher corporate taxes.

00:39:57.139 --> 00:39:59.414
Higher and higher levels of regulation.

00:39:59.414 --> 00:40:02.549
Higher and higher personal attacks.

00:40:02.549 --> 00:40:03.230
Burdens.

00:40:04.580 --> 00:40:06.880
Schools that are being run, more for

00:40:06.880 --> 00:40:09.630
the interest of the, the unions than they
are for the students.

00:40:10.650 --> 00:40:12.380
Permanent intractable poverty.

00:40:12.380 --> 00:40:15.060
Generational poverty is what I'm referring
to.

00:40:15.060 --> 00:40:18.240
These things give me a great deal of
concern.

00:40:18.240 --> 00:40:21.300
I, I'm concerned about America's
innovation lead.

00:40:21.300 --> 00:40:25.560
We, the, the political class doesn't
understand we're in competition.

00:40:26.620 --> 00:40:30.760
The political class thinks like the
American business world

00:40:30.760 --> 00:40:36.610
used to think back in the 1960s, which you
didn't.

00:40:36.610 --> 00:40:37.950
And then came along strategy.

00:40:37.950 --> 00:40:39.950
Which was the idea of not just how good I
am, but

00:40:39.950 --> 00:40:43.060
how good I am relative against everyone I
compete with.

00:40:43.060 --> 00:40:47.370
And right now we're competing and the
political class doesn't understand that

00:40:47.370 --> 00:40:51.308
we're competing with Russia, and with
China, and with the Jihadists.

00:40:51.308 --> 00:40:53.013
They have a strategy,

00:40:53.013 --> 00:40:57.694
Russia has a strategy China has a
strategy, and objectives.

00:40:57.694 --> 00:40:58.785
The jihadists do.

00:40:58.785 --> 00:41:00.950
What's America's strategy?

00:41:00.950 --> 00:41:02.060
What's our global strategy?

00:41:02.060 --> 00:41:03.210
Where are we headed?

00:41:03.210 --> 00:41:04.730
What are we trying to accomplish?

00:41:04.730 --> 00:41:08.280
What are we doing in Latin America, what's
our strategy for North Africa,

00:41:08.280 --> 00:41:09.260
the Middle East?

00:41:09.260 --> 00:41:11.550
Hilary Clinton said it right.

00:41:11.550 --> 00:41:14.704
Just deciding not to do dumb things is not
a strategy.

00:41:14.704 --> 00:41:19.053
And and so, I mean, that, that's what
concerns me most about America and

00:41:19.053 --> 00:41:23.804
about our economy is that we don't seem to
have a strategy about where we're heading.

00:41:23.804 --> 00:41:27.238
And we're becoming more and more like
Europe on a number of dimensions,

00:41:27.238 --> 00:41:30.631
education, poverty, taxation, regulation,
and foreign policy.

00:41:30.631 --> 00:41:34.134
And that, that gives me concern, and
that's why I'm as active as I am still,

00:41:34.134 --> 00:41:34.834
politically.

00:41:34.834 --> 00:41:36.761
Though I'm not running for office, thank
you very much

00:41:36.761 --> 00:41:39.872
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] We have a question over here.

00:41:39.872 --> 00:41:41.591
Stand up then, just introduce yourself
please.

00:41:41.591 --> 00:41:42.241
&gt;&gt; Hi.

00:41:42.241 --> 00:41:44.722
&gt;&gt; Hi Governor Romney, my name is Alex
Pierce, and

00:41:44.722 --> 00:41:48.530
I'm a first year all select [UNKNOWN] I'm
from Massachusetts as well.

00:41:48.530 --> 00:41:53.346
One of the central themes of the GSB, and
the view from the top program is

00:41:53.346 --> 00:41:58.490
the power of Authenticity as a tool for
effective leadership.

00:41:58.490 --> 00:42:02.683
We see then a business but we see that, I
think, less so in political leadership.

00:42:02.683 --> 00:42:07.659
And so I wanted to see how you balanced
being the authentic Mitt Romney versus

00:42:07.659 --> 00:42:12.636
what you thought the audience or the kind
of Republican machine wanted you to

00:42:12.636 --> 00:42:15.690
be like, or what, what did you wanted to
say?

00:42:16.860 --> 00:42:21.190
&gt;&gt; Yeah, you know, one of the great
challenges in running for office,

00:42:21.190 --> 00:42:23.260
is that you will be defined by your
opposition.

00:42:24.320 --> 00:42:27.970
And at and it and, by the way, an attack
is,

00:42:27.970 --> 00:42:33.720
is very difficult to respond to without
spending all your time in response.

00:42:33.720 --> 00:42:35.180
I remember the first time I got attacked,
and

00:42:35.180 --> 00:42:37.390
this was when I was running against Ted
Kennedy.

00:42:37.390 --> 00:42:41.580
And, and, and my, you know I went to my
staff and

00:42:41.580 --> 00:42:44.300
I said you know we got to answer these
charges these attacks.

00:42:44.300 --> 00:42:47.430
And they said you know, if you are
explaining you are losing.

00:42:48.890 --> 00:42:51.850
The, that is a little catch phrase in
politics,

00:42:51.850 --> 00:42:53.940
if you are explaining you are losing.

00:42:53.940 --> 00:42:56.510
So you never explain, you never respond to
the attacks.

00:42:56.510 --> 00:42:57.440
You just attack back.

00:42:57.440 --> 00:43:00.420
And the only thing you can do is attack
back just harder.

00:43:00.420 --> 00:43:02.830
And, and so these campaigns are just
attack, attack, attack.

00:43:02.830 --> 00:43:03.810
And by and large,

00:43:03.810 --> 00:43:09.460
it's very difficult in a campaign to have
people get to know who you really are.

00:43:09.460 --> 00:43:12.820
I mean, in a campaign you spent more time
with me by far.

00:43:12.820 --> 00:43:15.020
And I mean TV time included.

00:43:15.020 --> 00:43:18.596
If you take all the time you saw during
the 2012 campaign, of b, and

00:43:18.596 --> 00:43:22.527
compare it with what we spent in this
room, we spent more time now, together,

00:43:22.527 --> 00:43:25.078
talking about things that we all care
about.

00:43:25.078 --> 00:43:28.974
We choose candidates based on very little
interaction with them, and

00:43:28.974 --> 00:43:33.398
typically the interaction is watching 30
second ads, which are packaged and

00:43:33.398 --> 00:43:36.782
very brief, and by and large are attacking
their opponent.

00:43:36.782 --> 00:43:38.245
And, and then debates.

00:43:38.245 --> 00:43:40.049
And that's about it.

00:43:40.049 --> 00:43:43.245
Some people have noted, you know, Mitt, if
you'd have just shown that,

00:43:43.245 --> 00:43:45.426
that documentary that was done on you,
why, the,

00:43:45.426 --> 00:43:47.694
the people would've had a different
perspective.

00:43:47.694 --> 00:43:49.522
It's, like, yeah.
But who would've watched a documentary?

00:43:49.522 --> 00:43:50.242
All right?

00:43:50.242 --> 00:43:54.666
Who's gonna sit down and watch a one and a
half hour movie on someone running for

00:43:54.666 --> 00:43:55.211
office?

00:43:55.211 --> 00:43:58.882
I mean, no one's gonna do that cuz you're
gonna figure, rightly,

00:43:58.882 --> 00:44:03.688
it was all, you know, entered in for the
campaign, by the campaign, and, and so I,

00:44:03.688 --> 00:44:08.645
I I don't know how we can do a better job
describing and showing who we are.

00:44:08.645 --> 00:44:14.480
And, and my own view was, look, I was, I
served as Governor for four years.

00:44:14.480 --> 00:44:17.830
The positions I had and the postures I
took on issues

00:44:17.830 --> 00:44:20.600
were the same as when I was governor as
when I was running for president.

00:44:20.600 --> 00:44:24.330
And hopefully people will get that
perspective based on my record.

00:44:24.330 --> 00:44:28.150
But it's, it's a real challenge and, and
in the upcoming election

00:44:28.150 --> 00:44:31.570
presuming Hilary Clinton is the nominee on
the democratic side.

00:44:31.570 --> 00:44:34.390
And she may not be, but it looks obviously
very good for here now.

00:44:35.860 --> 00:44:39.870
She's pretty well defined in people's
minds, pro and con.

00:44:39.870 --> 00:44:43.190
On the Republican side, people aren't as
well defined.

00:44:43.190 --> 00:44:44.935
And so they're going to have a challenge,

00:44:44.935 --> 00:44:48.146
whoever our nominee is, becoming better
known in the mind of the public.

00:44:48.146 --> 00:44:52.599
And the goal of the opposition will be to
define the Republican in a negative light,

00:44:52.599 --> 00:44:56.750
in a very negative light, and that's just,
that's part of the territory.

00:44:56.750 --> 00:44:57.820
You know, getting into a race.

00:44:57.820 --> 00:45:01.395
It's, it's, it, it, you, I was gonna say
it's not fun.

00:45:01.395 --> 00:45:03.333
The truth is it is fun.

00:45:03.333 --> 00:45:04.842
Running for President is really fun.

00:45:04.842 --> 00:45:06.202
It's like for sport for old guys.

00:45:06.202 --> 00:45:08.014
All right?
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:45:08.014 --> 00:45:09.384
&gt;&gt; It's, and

00:45:09.384 --> 00:45:13.271
you come away, passionate about a coun,
the country,

00:45:13.271 --> 00:45:17.790
more optimistic about the country, you
meet extraordinary people.

00:45:17.790 --> 00:45:19.020
It's a wonderful experience.

00:45:19.020 --> 00:45:21.130
If you get the chance to run for
president, do it for sure.

00:45:22.198 --> 00:45:24.828
But I love the experience.

00:45:24.828 --> 00:45:29.264
And, and, you know, I just wish in the
campaign, you had more time to be seen and

00:45:29.264 --> 00:45:32.071
known by more people and I think if people
did that,

00:45:32.071 --> 00:45:36.611
we'd have a better shot at electing people
we were happy with after the election.

00:45:36.611 --> 00:45:37.238
&gt;&gt; Yeah, thanks.

00:45:37.238 --> 00:45:39.583
&gt;&gt; Great, we have one question over here
in the back.

00:45:39.583 --> 00:45:40.535
&gt;&gt; Okay, yes sir.

00:45:40.535 --> 00:45:43.417
&gt;&gt; Hey, my name's Elliot Damachek, and I'm
a second-year MBA.

00:45:43.417 --> 00:45:47.417
Was at Bain Consulting beforehand and
going back afterwards, so thank you for

00:45:47.417 --> 00:45:48.802
helping to make that exist.

00:45:48.802 --> 00:45:51.389
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] My question is around you

00:45:51.389 --> 00:45:56.233
mentioned that your father was helped by
public assistance programs a while back,

00:45:56.233 --> 00:46:00.524
and traditionally the view of the
Republican party is much less favorable

00:46:00.524 --> 00:46:02.895
towards those types of programs.

00:46:02.895 --> 00:46:06.635
So I was wondering how your view on that
has evolved and

00:46:06.635 --> 00:46:08.885
how it's shaped by your father's
experience.

00:46:08.885 --> 00:46:09.889
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
Thank you.

00:46:09.889 --> 00:46:15.188
A,a, actually, there are, I, I gotta talk
about two groups of, of People in poverty.

00:46:15.188 --> 00:46:17.558
One I'll call situational poverty.

00:46:17.558 --> 00:46:22.550
Someone loses a job, or, or has a life
changing illness of some kind.

00:46:22.550 --> 00:46:25.475
And the situation means, they're now in
poverty.

00:46:25.475 --> 00:46:29.265
Those people typically come out of poverty
relatively quicker.

00:46:29.265 --> 00:46:31.866
When I say relatively quick, they come out
of poverty eventually.

00:46:31.866 --> 00:46:36.190
And, and the programs to help them come
out of poverty are extraordinarily helpful

00:46:36.190 --> 00:46:37.068
and important.

00:46:37.068 --> 00:46:43.990
And it could be housing, food, training
programs, child care and so forth.

00:46:43.990 --> 00:46:50.140
And we have a good series of social help
programs for people in that setting.

00:46:50.140 --> 00:46:51.130
I'm sure they could get better.

00:46:51.130 --> 00:46:53.200
Unemployment insurance is another of
those.

00:46:53.200 --> 00:46:55.520
And they should be fine tuned and

00:46:55.520 --> 00:46:58.190
evaluated to see what makes them more
effective, but those work well.

00:46:59.340 --> 00:47:03.681
Where, where Republicans like myself have
some angst is in our solutions for

00:47:03.681 --> 00:47:08.225
generational property, and that's people
who obviously go from generation to

00:47:08.225 --> 00:47:11.750
generation in poverty and don't ever get
out.

00:47:11.750 --> 00:47:12.720
Back in the 1960s,

00:47:12.720 --> 00:47:17.820
when I was in high school, Lyndon Johnson
declared the War on Poverty.

00:47:18.850 --> 00:47:23.500
We today have record levels of poverty,
more than when Johnson was president.

00:47:23.500 --> 00:47:25.900
About 15% of Americans live in poverty.

00:47:25.900 --> 00:47:28.030
So, so, why didn't it work?

00:47:28.030 --> 00:47:30.370
And the answer is the programs that were
put into place,

00:47:30.370 --> 00:47:33.460
in many respects, made it more difficult
for many people to get out of poverty.

00:47:33.460 --> 00:47:37.690
I mean, the highest marginal tax rate in
America is not the marginal tax rate for

00:47:37.690 --> 00:47:39.250
people in my tax bracket or yours.

00:47:40.530 --> 00:47:42.610
The highest marginal tax rate is for the
poor.

00:47:42.610 --> 00:47:47.447
If you're on Medicaid, housing vouchers,
food stamps,

00:47:47.447 --> 00:47:50.843
and so forth and you start earning money.

00:47:50.843 --> 00:47:53.258
You're gonna lose all sorts of benefits.

00:47:53.258 --> 00:47:57.577
And the effective marginal tax rate if you
will, benefit tax rate, is huge!

00:47:57.577 --> 00:48:01.304
And so we lock people from in, in, into,
into staying, on,

00:48:01.304 --> 00:48:06.030
on, on government assistance because it
would be crazy for them to get off.

00:48:06.030 --> 00:48:09.050
We make it almost impossible to get
married.

00:48:09.050 --> 00:48:10.410
If, if he, for a, a poor person.

00:48:10.410 --> 00:48:16.650
If a young person is pregnant and, and she
decides to marry the father of the child,

00:48:16.650 --> 00:48:20.360
she's far less likely to qualify for
Medicaid, for housing vouchers, for

00:48:20.360 --> 00:48:22.130
food stamps, and other forms of
assistance.

00:48:22.130 --> 00:48:27.067
The, the only likely way that she'll be
able to really get the support she needs

00:48:27.067 --> 00:48:30.603
is if the father provides some of his
support that he has,

00:48:30.603 --> 00:48:36.630
doesn't get married and she gets Medicaid
and so forth, these other programs.

00:48:36.630 --> 00:48:42.140
And as a result, not surprisingly, huge
numbers of people follow that path.

00:48:42.140 --> 00:48:46.160
There, there was a study done by Brookings
Institution some years ago, and,

00:48:46.160 --> 00:48:48.180
and I've got the numbers here
directionally right.

00:48:48.180 --> 00:48:51.210
I'll have to go back and look at the
numbers, but they're close to these.

00:48:51.210 --> 00:48:54.750
They said, what, what happens in America
if someone graduates from high school,

00:48:55.770 --> 00:49:01.380
marries before they have their first child
and has ever held a job, and

00:49:01.380 --> 00:49:04.030
the answer is 3% of those people will fall
in poverty.

00:49:05.350 --> 00:49:08.015
What happens if someone doesn't do any of
those three things?

00:49:08.015 --> 00:49:11.380
70% of them will be in poverty.

00:49:11.380 --> 00:49:13.200
It's like aha, so

00:49:13.200 --> 00:49:16.400
if you want to make sure we eliminate
generational poverty in America,

00:49:16.400 --> 00:49:19.999
one you want to make sure there are huge
incentives to get people their first job.

00:49:21.050 --> 00:49:22.300
We don't do that.

00:49:22.300 --> 00:49:26.100
Two, you wanna make sure that schools are
safe and teachers are effective so

00:49:26.100 --> 00:49:27.500
we can get people through high school.

00:49:27.500 --> 00:49:28.570
We don't do that.

00:49:28.570 --> 00:49:32.320
And three, you wanna make sure that your
government programs encourage people to

00:49:32.320 --> 00:49:36.340
get married as opposed to create
disincentives for marriage, which ours do.

00:49:36.340 --> 00:49:40.490
We're doing exactly the wrong things if we
wanna get people out of poverty.

00:49:40.490 --> 00:49:47.130
And yet there doesn't seem to be any real
effort in Washington to change that.

00:49:47.130 --> 00:49:49.400
So Republicans are saying, by the way,
when I was Governor,

00:49:49.400 --> 00:49:53.020
I said look I want to spend more money on
childcare and have a greater work

00:49:55.110 --> 00:49:57.750
requirement to get people out of the home
and into, into the workplace.

00:49:57.750 --> 00:50:01.140
I am happy to spend more money on
assistance but

00:50:01.140 --> 00:50:05.778
I want it to help get people out of
poverty, not lock them into poverty.

00:50:05.778 --> 00:50:10.280
So support and, and government assistance
to end and

00:50:10.280 --> 00:50:14.330
alleviate poverty, Republicans are all in
favor of, but they want to find things

00:50:14.330 --> 00:50:19.498
that actually solve the problem as opposed
to cause people to live in poverty in a,

00:50:19.498 --> 00:50:23.110
in a human tragedy that goes on and on and
on, generation to generation.

00:50:23.110 --> 00:50:25.770
Thanks for the question.

00:50:25.770 --> 00:50:28.780
&gt;&gt; Unfortunately I, I'm gonna have to take
the last question here.

00:50:28.780 --> 00:50:29.800
&gt;&gt; Boy, I am wordy aren't I?
&gt;&gt; We're cutting you off, yeah.

00:50:29.800 --> 00:50:30.300
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Be worried.

00:50:30.300 --> 00:50:30.840
No we we asked a,

00:50:30.840 --> 00:50:33.300
a question of all of you from the top
speakers, and

00:50:33.300 --> 00:50:38.595
its one that we have to answer when we
apply to the school.

00:50:38.595 --> 00:50:43.450
And there are obviously a lot of ways you
can answer this so I'm interested, but

00:50:43.450 --> 00:50:45.470
the question is what matters most to you
and why?

00:50:47.600 --> 00:50:49.350
&gt;&gt; It's not one thing.

00:50:50.590 --> 00:50:52.490
And.
So I'm going to give you a longer answer.

00:50:52.490 --> 00:50:53.500
One, another long answer.

00:50:53.500 --> 00:50:55.300
One, I believe in God.

00:50:55.300 --> 00:50:56.380
I know some people don't.

00:50:56.380 --> 00:51:01.313
Many people who don't, nonetheless,
believe in something

00:51:01.313 --> 00:51:06.210
greater than themselves.

00:51:06.210 --> 00:51:10.030
I, I believe that, that if you believe in
something greater than yourself, that,

00:51:10.030 --> 00:51:13.020
that your life will be more full and and
productive.

00:51:13.020 --> 00:51:14.300
So I, I believe in God.

00:51:14.300 --> 00:51:18.550
And by the way, in believing in God, I
believe therefore we are all his children.

00:51:18.550 --> 00:51:21.950
And I believe that God loves all of us and

00:51:21.950 --> 00:51:25.340
I believe he loves us as you would love
your children.

00:51:25.340 --> 00:51:26.570
Some are doing naughty things.

00:51:26.570 --> 00:51:27.450
Some are doing nice things.

00:51:27.450 --> 00:51:29.090
But you love them all.

00:51:29.090 --> 00:51:33.900
And and, and I believe that I will be
measured, and you will be measured,

00:51:33.900 --> 00:51:39.000
based upon what you have done for your
fellow, children of God.

00:51:39.000 --> 00:51:43.270
And and that means your spouse, your
children, and your community more broadly.

00:51:45.166 --> 00:51:47.489
So that's a big part of what's important
to me,

00:51:47.489 --> 00:51:50.200
which are the people around me that I care
for.

00:51:50.200 --> 00:51:53.110
The person I care for moot, most in life
is my wife.

00:51:53.110 --> 00:51:54.520
We met at a high school.

00:51:54.520 --> 00:51:55.830
I love her passionately.

00:51:55.830 --> 00:51:58.240
She is the most important person in my
life.

00:51:58.240 --> 00:52:02.020
If I could do anything, any day, it would
be to be with her.

00:52:02.020 --> 00:52:04.050
That's what I enjoy most in life.

00:52:04.050 --> 00:52:06.440
Close thereafter is to be with my kids.

00:52:06.440 --> 00:52:10.410
My, my boys and their wives, and now 23
grandkids.

00:52:10.410 --> 00:52:14.150
The, the greatest joy I have in life is
being with them.

00:52:14.150 --> 00:52:17.940
Sitting around in the back yard, or at a
beach just being with my grandkids and

00:52:17.940 --> 00:52:19.720
family, is the greatest source of
happiness and

00:52:19.720 --> 00:52:21.740
the most important thing to me.

00:52:21.740 --> 00:52:25.340
Coming beyond that, is a circle which
includes my church, my congregation,

00:52:25.340 --> 00:52:27.949
the people I know at church, and my sense
of service to them.

00:52:29.650 --> 00:52:31.320
Service in the broadest sense.

00:52:32.604 --> 00:52:35.959
Giving back in the sense of caring for
people around me is,

00:52:35.959 --> 00:52:40.510
is follows from that belief in something
bigger than myself.

00:52:40.510 --> 00:52:43.985
I, I happen to believe that the currency
in life is the people that you love and

00:52:43.985 --> 00:52:45.360
that care for you.

00:52:45.360 --> 00:52:46.930
The friends you have.

00:52:46.930 --> 00:52:48.970
Most of which you've learned here, you'll
forget.

00:52:48.970 --> 00:52:52.600
The people you met here, you'll remember
for the rest of your life and and

00:52:52.600 --> 00:52:54.840
will form a big part of your, of your
wealth.

00:52:54.840 --> 00:52:58.190
That's your balance sheet when life is
over, who loves you and you love, and

00:52:58.190 --> 00:53:01.730
who are your friends and, and and how
close are they to you.

00:53:01.730 --> 00:53:03.580
So what's the most important thing to me?

00:53:03.580 --> 00:53:08.480
My God, my, my wife, my kids and, and my
fellow human beings.

00:53:08.480 --> 00:53:13.490
And, and I participate and engage with,
with, with people large through campaigns,

00:53:13.490 --> 00:53:18.750
through speeches, through serving at
charities in all sorts of ways.

00:53:18.750 --> 00:53:24.590
And, and the rewards, the rewards that
come back are, are rewards in people.

00:53:24.590 --> 00:53:30.369
And in friendship and love, and that's
what makes the most impact in my life.

00:53:30.369 --> 00:53:31.859
Thanks, you guys.
Congratulations to you and best of luck.

00:53:31.859 --> 00:53:33.368
Good to be with you
&gt;&gt; Thank you very much.

00:53:33.368 --> 00:53:34.158
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:53:34.158 --> 00:53:34.947
Thank you.

00:53:34.947 --> 00:53:35.667
Thank you.

00:53:35.667 --> 00:53:36.178
Nicely done.

00:53:36.178 --> 00:53:37.037
Very nicely done.

